Subject: Complete the GPU blit before a screen-cast dma-buf is queued

The NVIDIA driver attaches no implicit fence to dma-bufs it renders into
(measured on 580.178.04: DMA_BUF_IOCTL_EXPORT_SYNC_FILE reports a fence as
signaled right after the blit is flushed, while the GPU still has tens of
milliseconds of work pending). Mutter only flushes before it queues the
buffer to PipeWire, so a consumer that reads it on the GPU while the GPU is
busy sees the buffer's previous contents, up to a whole ring of 16 buffers
old. With a busy GPU the remote picture jumps between the current frame and
frames from seconds ago, then slowly catches up.

Finish the blit before the buffer is queued. In a two-process reproduction a
consumer read stale pixels in 9 of 10 trials with a flush and in 0 of 10 with
a finish. Cost: the compositor waits for its own blit, once per recorded
frame, only while a screen cast is running.

--- a/src/backends/meta-screen-cast-stream-src.c
+++ b/src/backends/meta-screen-cast-stream-src.c
@@ -631,10 +631,23 @@
       COGL_TRACE_BEGIN_SCOPED (RecordToFramebuffer,
                                "Meta::ScreenCastStreamSrc::record_to_framebuffer()");
 
-      return meta_screen_cast_stream_src_record_to_framebuffer (src,
-                                                                paint_phase,
-                                                                dmabuf_fbo,
-                                                                error);
+      if (!meta_screen_cast_stream_src_record_to_framebuffer (src,
+                                                              paint_phase,
+                                                              dmabuf_fbo,
+                                                              error))
+        return FALSE;
+
+      /* The buffer is queued to PipeWire right after this returns, and the
+       * consumer reads it on the GPU. The NVIDIA driver attaches no implicit
+       * fence to dma-bufs it renders into, so a consumer cannot tell that the
+       * blit above is still pending: under GPU load it reads whatever this
+       * buffer held the last time it was used, which is up to a full ring of
+       * buffers (16 frames) old. Wait for the GPU here so a queued buffer is
+       * always complete.
+       */
+      cogl_framebuffer_finish (dmabuf_fbo);
+
+      return TRUE;
     }
 
   g_set_error (error, G_IO_ERROR, G_IO_ERROR_FAILED,
